sum='“The Fun They Had” is a science-fiction story by Isaac Asimov set in the year 2157. Eleven-year-old Margie writes in her diary that Tommy (13) found a real book – an old, printed book about school. Children of that time read only “telebooks” on screens, so the yellow, crinkly pages and words that stood still surprised them. Margie hates her school: a mechanical teacher (a large, black, ugly computer with a screen and a slot for homework and test papers) set up in a room next to her bedroom; she must study alone at the same time every day because her mother believes little girls learn better at regular hours. The County Inspector adjusts the geography sector when Margie does badly. Tommy explains that in the old days all children of the neighbourhood went to a special building, learned the same things together from a human teacher, helped each other with homework and talked about it. Margie thinks of how much fun the children must have had. In “The Road Not Taken”, Robert Frost’s traveller stands where two roads diverge in a yellow wood. Both look almost equally worn, but he chooses the one that seems grassier and less used, saving the first “for another day”, though he knows he will probably never return. He imagines telling, years later, with a sigh, that he took the road less travelled and that it made all the difference – our choices shape our lives.',
