 (21, 'The Road Not Taken – Thinking about the Poem', [
  'I. 1. He finds himself in a yellow (autumn) wood where the road divides into two. His problem: he must choose one road, as he cannot travel both.',
  '2. (i) a forest in autumn with yellow leaves (ii) the road was covered with grass and seemed less used (iii) people walking on the road (iv) fresh fallen leaves not yet walked on and blackened by footsteps (v) one road leads to another and we rarely come back to the same point.',
  '3. (i) In stanzas two and three there is hardly any difference – both roads were equally worn and covered with untrodden leaves. (ii) In the last two lines the poet calls his road “the one less travelled by” – a difference made by his choice.',
  '4. The poet’s choice of the less-travelled road changed his whole life. He accepts it; the “sigh” shows he thinks about the other possibility, but he does not really regret.',
  'II. 1–2. (Personal answer) e.g., choosing between science and arts / a new school – I will consider my interest, ability and advice of parents and teachers. After making a choice, I accept it and work hard instead of worrying about “what might have been”.']),
